<Project>

  <!--
    Central Package Management: every PackageReference version lives here, and the .csproj
    files carry only <PackageReference Include="..." /> with no Version attribute.

    Why: the same package was pinned at three different versions across the test projects
    (xunit 2.5.3 / 2.6.2 / 2.9.0, xunit.runner.visualstudio 2.5.3 / 2.5.4 / 2.8.2,
    Microsoft.NET.Test.Sdk 17.8.0 / 17.12.0), and coverlet.collector was missing from two of
    them — so any coverage figure silently excluded those projects. One version per package
    removes that whole class of drift.
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <ManagePackageVersionsCentrally>true</ManagePackageVersionsCentrally>
    <!-- Fail the build if a project tries to pin its own version without VersionOverride. -->
    <CentralPackageTransitivePinningEnabled>true</CentralPackageTransitivePinningEnabled>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup Label="Rendering / text shaping">
    <!--
      Stable line. This was pinned to 4.147.0-preview.3.1 until 2026-08-04; the 4.147 line never
      shipped a stable release, so a published package forced prerelease resolution on every
      consumer. 4.148.0 was the first stable, and 4.151.0 is current. Keep the SkiaSharp entries
      in lockstep.

      Native assets are referenced for all three desktop platforms, so the published packages do
      not force a Windows-only dependency graph on consumers. Only Win32 was referenced until
      2026-08-04, which is what made the library Windows-only in practice; macOS was already
      arriving transitively, so Linux was the sole real gap.
    -->
    <PackageVersion Include="SkiaSharp" Version="4.156.1" />
    <PackageVersion Include="SkiaSharp.NativeAssets.Win32" Version="4.156.1" />
    <PackageVersion Include="SkiaSharp.NativeAssets.Linux" Version="4.156.1" />
    <PackageVersion Include="SkiaSharp.NativeAssets.macOS" Version="4.156.1" />
    <!--
      DO NOT ADD HARFBUZZ BACK, and in particular not "to support Arabic/Hebrew". SkiaSharp.HarfBuzz
      and HarfBuzzSharp.NativeAssets.{Linux,macOS} were removed on 2026-08-07 along with their only
      consumer, Base/Rendering/TextShaper.cs, which had no callers anywhere in the tree.

      Why not: a PDF *reader* must not shape. The producer already chose the glyphs, and a
      non-embedded Arabic file stores presentation forms (U+FE70-FEFF) that a second shaping pass
      would corrupt. The producer side (Builder, DocxToPdf, AcroForm appearances) shapes through the
      pure-managed Core.Text.OpenTypeShaper, which needs no native library. See CLAUDE.md and the
      CHANGELOG entry for 2026-08-07.

      If they ever do return, the versioning trap that justified them is this: HarfBuzzSharp
      versions on its own line - 14.2.1, NOT SkiaSharp's 4.151.0 - and it ships libHarfBuzzSharp
      separately from libSkiaSharp, so referencing SkiaSharp.HarfBuzz without the two NativeAssets
      packages yields a package that restores cleanly and dies at the first shaped run.
    -->
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup Label="Cryptography">
    <!--
      2.7.0, and the reason it was previously refused is now understood and fixed rather than
      worked around. The earlier round measured 361/0 -> 308/53 under 2.7.0, dominated by
      `Org.BouncyCastle.Cms.CmsException: Exception reading content`, and recorded that it was
      "not obviously the whole cause". It was the whole cause.

      **2.7.0 refuses trailing bytes after the ASN.1 object, and a PDF /Contents is always
      padded.** PdfSigner writes the CMS into a fixed SigningDefaults.SignatureReserveBytes hole
      and right-pads the hex with '0', so every signature this library produces is followed by
      thousands of zero bytes; 2.6.2 ignores them and 2.7.0 answers
      `IOException: extra data found after object`. The four failing areas were exactly the four
      call sites handing PdfString.RawBytes to a CMS or token parser. "The signature did not
      verify over the signed byte range" was a PARSE failure wearing a verification failure's
      clothes.

      Fixed by SigningDefaults.TrimToAsn1Object at all four. Measured before taking this bump:
      the full solution is 10959/0/12 on 2.6.2 AND on 2.7.0, identical; the Encryption suite is
      green on both; the weak-algorithm mutations still kill on both; and a signature produced
      under either version verifies under the other, tested in both directions.

      **If you are bisecting a signature problem, revert this line to 2.6.2 first** - it is the
      one-line experiment, and the trim is correct under both versions. GAPS.md §20c carries the
      measurements and what is still open (a non-zero /Contents tail is unsigned and is still not
      refused - that is a policy decision, not an oversight).
    -->
    <PackageVersion Include="BouncyCastle.Cryptography" Version="2.7.0" />
    <!--
      SignedCms / CmsSigner, for Plugins.CertStoreSign. NOT in-box on any leg: on .NET it lives in
      a separate System.Security.Cryptography.Pkcs assembly that must be referenced explicitly.
      It is the only way to build a CMS with a key that stays on a smart card, which BouncyCastle
      cannot do — it needs an AsymmetricKeyParameter, and a non-exportable CNG key cannot produce
      one. Referenced by that plugin alone, which is why the plugin exists rather than the code
      living in Tetitulz.Pdf.Signing.
      10.0.13 (2026-10-15): 10.0.0-10.0.12 carry GHSA-846f-f764-76mx (CVE-2026-84008, high: quadratic
      CPU in SignedCms.CheckSignature on attacker-supplied certificates) and GHSA-2fmv-rv2c-59x7
      (CVE-2026-70162, moderate: quadratic work in EnvelopedCms.Decode on Windows); NuGet's audit
      flagged both on restore. A shipped package's dependency is every consumer's floor, so this stays
      the lowest version carrying those fixes; a consumer may take a later one.
    -->
    <PackageVersion Include="System.Security.Cryptography.Pkcs" Version="10.0.13" />
    <!-- netstandard2.0/net462 compile-time only; assets excluded so it never ships at runtime. -->
    <PackageVersion Include="Portable.BouncyCastle" Version="1.9.0" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup Label="Document formats">
    <!-- 1.8.x because the AngleSharp.Js 1.1.0 source Plugins.HtmlScript vendors (Wave 16) is
         written against [1.8.0, 2.0.0). -->
    <PackageVersion Include="AngleSharp" Version="1.8.2" />
    <!-- Plugins.HtmlScript only. AngleSharp.Css supplies element.style (the CSSOM): without it
         jQuery 1.x throws in its own feature detection and 3.x drops every .css() silently. -->
    <PackageVersion Include="AngleSharp.Css" Version="1.1.2" />
    <!-- Tests only (Plugins.HtmlScript.Tests): the official package, beside the plugin's vendored
         copy, proves a host can reference both - same source version as the vendored one. -->
    <PackageVersion Include="AngleSharp.Js" Version="1.1.0" />
    <PackageVersion Include="DocumentFormat.OpenXml" Version="3.5.1" />
    <!-- 1.64.1 at least: Plugins.HtmlBrowser routes a page's WebSockets through its address
         guard, and up to 1.62.0 the .NET client reads a close code the browser may omit, so one
         routed socket fails every later call on the connection (KeyNotFoundException). -->
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.Playwright" Version="1.64.1" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Tesseract" Version="5.2.0" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup Label="Scripting / blockchain">
    <!--
      Jint belongs to Tetitulz.Pdf.Plugins.JavaScript ONLY (moved there 2026-08-12, GAPS.md §15b).
      It was a Tetitulz.Pdf.Base PackageReference reached from a single 449-line file, which put a
      full ECMAScript interpreter into the package graph of every consumer of Base - all four
      feature DLLs, most plugins, and the WinForms viewer's net462 leg. Do NOT add it back to Base
      to spare a caller the plugin registration; AssemblyLayeringTests fails the build if you do.
    -->
    <!-- Also Plugins.HtmlScript (Wave 16), which vendors AngleSharp.Js 1.1.0 (it wants Jint
         [4.15.3, 5.0.0)) - see that plugin's VENDORED.md before moving this pin. -->
    <PackageVersion Include="Jint" Version="4.18.1" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Nethereum.Web3" Version="6.1.0" />
    <!-- Pinned to 13.0.3 to avoid GHSA-5crp-9r3c-p9vr in older transitive versions. -->
    <PackageVersion Include="Newtonsoft.Json" Version="13.0.4" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup Label="Runtime / BCL">
    <!-- The `dynamic` binder. Referenced ONLY by Tetitulz.Pdf.DocxToPdf's netstandard2.0 leg, for
         WordComConverter's late-bound COM calls: net10.0 carries the binder in the shared framework,
         netstandard2.0 does not, and without it `dynamic` fails to compile with CS0656. -->
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.CSharp" Version="4.7.0" />
    <!-- Span<T> / MemoryExtensions for netstandard2.0 legs. Referenced explicitly by
         Plugins.PostScript, which was relying on it arriving transitively through
         Core -> System.Text.Encoding.CodePages. 4.6.3 matches the pin in
         tools/package-consumption/Consumer; a different assembly version there produced a
         FileLoadException on .NET Framework, so keep the two in step. -->
    <PackageVersion Include="System.Memory" Version="4.6.3" />
    <!-- Core's code pages (and PostScript's). Every package on Core requires at least this version,
         so it is the lowest one the library needs, not the newest: a consumer pinning an earlier
         major would otherwise fail to restore. Raised for a fix the library needs. -->
    <PackageVersion Include="System.Text.Encoding.CodePages" Version="10.0.11" />
    <!-- Transitive-vulnerability overrides: GHSA-7jgj-8wvc-jh57, GHSA-cmhx-cq75-c4mj.

         KEPT ON PURPOSE, AND NO LONGER FOR THE ORIGINAL REASON. Four test projects used to carry
         a direct PackageReference to each of these purely to force a safe version; on net10.0 both
         ship in the shared framework, so NuGet prunes the out-of-band package and warned about it
         (NU1510). Those references are gone (2026-08-27), and a transitive package listing now
         finds neither package anywhere in the tree.

         These two lines are not the leftovers of that — they are the guard that outlives it.
         CentralPackageTransitivePinningEnabled is true above, so a PackageVersion with no
         PackageReference is inert *until* some future dependency drags the package in
         transitively, at which point it pins the safe version instead of the vulnerable one.
         Deleting them would remove a guard that costs nothing and currently has no prey.

         Do not re-add a PackageReference for either: that is what NU1510 was complaining about. -->
    <PackageVersion Include="System.Net.Http" Version="4.3.4" />
    <PackageVersion Include="System.Text.RegularExpressions" Version="4.3.1" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup Label="Test infrastructure (converged — was three different sets)">
    <PackageVersion Include="coverlet.collector" Version="10.1.1" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.NET.Test.Sdk" Version="18.12.0" />
    <PackageVersion Include="xunit" Version="2.9.3" />
    <PackageVersion Include="xunit.runner.visualstudio" Version="4.0.1" />
    <!-- The compiler's semantic model, for a guard that must see types a text search cannot:
         SaturatingCastGuardTests (Base.Tests) finds every float-to-integer cast in src/. -->
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.CSharp" Version="5.12.0" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup Label="Benchmarking">
    <!--
      Used only by benchmarks/Tetitulz.Pdf.Benchmarks, which is outside src/ so it never
      packs and none of the four CI guards apply to it. It is in tetitulz.pdf.sln (so it
      cannot rot) but deliberately NOT in tetitulz.pdf.portable.slnf, so the Linux CI job
      does not spend time building a harness it never runs.
    -->
    <PackageVersion Include="BenchmarkDotNet" Version="0.15.8" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup Label="Fuzzing">
    <!--
      Used only by tools/fuzz/Tetitulz.Pdf.Fuzz, the coverage-guided harness (GAPS.md section 7c),
      which is outside src/ and not in either solution file: its assemblies are instrumented in
      place by the sharpfuzz tool and run under libfuzzer-dotnet, never shipped.
    -->
    <PackageVersion Include="SharpFuzz" Version="2.3.0" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup Label="Build-time only">
    <PackageVersion Include="MinVer" Version="8.0.0" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
